  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Sawadaea tulasnei is a fungal pathogen belonging to the Powdery Mildews family (Erysiphaceae) that affects various plant hosts throughout New England. This obligate parasite forms characteristic white, powdery coating on plant surfaces and produces distinctive fruiting structures.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "White Powdery Coating",
      "description": "Visible as a fine, whitish powder covering affected leaves, stems, and sometimes flowers",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Perithecial Structures",
      "description": "Produces dark, round fruiting bodies (perithecia) with appendages that become visible as disease matures",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Specificity",
      "description": "Typically found on specific plant species within the region, particularly affecting woody plants and shrubs",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Sawadaea tulasnei thrives in New England's cool, humid environments, particularly in shaded areas with moderate moisture where host plants are present.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many powdery mildews that overwinter as spores, Sawadaea tulasnei produces hardened fruiting structures called perithecia that allow it to survive harsh New England winters and release spores in spring.",
